But observed shows after pressing ArrowDown, we got aria-activedescendant still "Market Street"? Actually observed says after step 2: aria-activedescendant observed "Market Street". So they expected it to become autoCompleteJS_result_0 but actually remained Market Street? Wait initial state maybe had some previous selection? Let's examine observed details.

Observed failedSteps includes step index 2 (the ArrowDown press). It gives before and after states.

Before: input has no aria-activedescendant attribute (empty). After: input has aria-activedescendant="Market Street". That suggests after pressing ArrowDown, the script set aria-activedescendant to the value of the first item? But why would it be "Market Street"? Possibly because the list items have dataset.value set to the item's name? In component Item.js, they set data-value attribute to item.value[item.key]; then later navigation sets aria-activedescendant to list[currentFocus].dataset.value. So the value stored is the item's name (like "Market Street").

Thus after pressing ArrowDown, they expected the first suggestion (index 0) to be highlighted, which corresponds to "Marina Park"? Wait the data list: src contains objects with name: 'Market Street', 'Marina Park', 'Maple Avenue', 'Harbour Mall', 'Station Square'. When typing "ma", the filtered results likely are those starting with "ma": Marina Park, Maple Avenue? Also maybe "Market Street"? Actually "ma" lower case matches "market street"? "market street".startsWith("ma")? Yes "market street" starts with "ma". So results include Market Street, Marina Park, Maple Avenue. Order? Probably as per original order: Market Street, Marina Park, Maple Avenue, Harbour Mall, Station Square. Filtering yields those three in same order: Market Street (index0), Marina Park (index1), Maple Avenue (index2). So first suggestion is Market Street. However the test expectation was that after typing "ma", the first suggestion should be Marina Park? Let's see step1 assertion: expects #autoCompleteJS_result_1 text "Marina Park". They refer to result_1 (zero-indexed?) They expect the second result (index 1) to be Marina Park. That suggests they consider result_0 is something else (maybe Market Street). Then after ArrowDown they expect aria-selected on result_0 (Market Street) true, and aria-activedescendant = result_0. That matches expectation: first ArrowDown highlights the first result (Market Street). However observed after ArrowDown gave aria-activedescendant = Market Street (which is correct?), but they said assertion failed because observed value was "Market Street" while expected was "autoCompleteJS_result_0". Wait the assertion is about attribute aria-activedescendant on #store expecting value "autoCompleteJS_result_0". But they observed "Market Street". Indeed the script sets aria-activedescendant to dataset.value (the item's name), not the element's id. The test expects it to be the element's id (autoCompleteJS_result_0). So the bug is that the navigation controller sets aria-act